---
description: Wrap PHP text/icon output in HTML tags as three clean lines — open, PHP, close
alwaysApply: true
---

# PHP inside HTML tags (three lines)

When a tag’s content is a PHP print (text, icon, etc.), do **not** put PHP on the same line as the tag. Open the tag, then PHP on the next line, then close the tag.

The opening tag itself stays **one line** (attributes stay inline).

## Do

```html
<span class="text-sm md:text-base font-normal text-cynTextPrimary leading-6">
    <?php esc_html_e('امکانات انتخابی', 'novavilla'); ?>
</span>
```

```html
<i class="size-6 flex items-center justify-center [&_svg]:size-full [&_svg]:stroke-[1.5]">
    <?php Icon::print('Arrow-27'); ?>
</i>
```

```html
<span>
    <?php echo esc_html($feature['title']); ?>
</span>
```

## Don't

```html
<!-- ❌ PHP jammed on the same line as the tag -->
<span class="text-sm"><?php esc_html_e('امکانات انتخابی', 'novavilla'); ?></span>
<i class="size-6"><?php Icon::print('Arrow-27'); ?></i>
```

## Applies when

- Text: `esc_html_e`, `echo esc_html(...)`, `the_title()`, etc. as the tag’s only content
- Icons: `Icon::print(...)` inside `<i>` (or similar)

## Does not apply

- PHP in **attributes** stays inline: `href="<?php echo esc_url($url); ?>"`
- Short self-contained PHP calls that are not wrapping tag content
- Opening-tag attributes — keep those on one line (compact style)
